WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.880
- Maybe you've seen this warning lately.

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:06.000
It's not good, especially
for your phone's battery.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.110
And...

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:08.850
But how hot is too hot?

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:09.683
Action.

00:00:09.683 --> 00:00:11.550
There was only one way to find out.

00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.763
Put some phones under a
food heat lamp, of course.

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.550
And 137 degrees.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.110
And there's the screen.

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:27.090
Please do not do this at
home for many reasons,

00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:30.960
but mainly, heat is really
bad for your phone's battery.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.310
And well, you may have heard.

00:00:32.310 --> 00:00:34.170
- You know, the heat is not backing off.

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:36.120
- And that heat is not going away.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:36.953
- Heat.
- Heat.

00:00:36.953 --> 00:00:38.700
- And combine that environmental heat

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.680
with processor intensive tasks

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:42.450
and even fast charging

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.510
and the phone temps
are only going to rise.

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:47.550
So why do batteries hate heat?

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.190
And what temperature triggers the warning?

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.560
And is there anything you can do about it?

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.283
And is having a mini phone fan cool?

00:00:57.330 --> 00:00:58.920
All right, let's heat up some phones

00:00:58.920 --> 00:01:00.690
and answer those questions.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:02.310
Okay, first thing to answer,

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:05.280
why is heat so bad for batteries?

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.580
This is a lithium ion battery
from inside an iPhone.

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.110
I'll spare you the chemistry class,

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:12.300
but there's a complicated
chemical reaction

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.750
that occurs during
charging and discharging.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.400
As you increase the temperature,

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:21.000
the speed of that chemical
reaction also increases,

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.370
that can cause the battery to drain faster

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:25.200
and the actual battery components

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:28.290
and capacity to degrade over time.

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.030
You can check the status of your iPhone

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:33.480
or Android phone's battery
capacity under settings.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:36.300
But how hot is too hot
for a smartphone battery?

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:38.790
95 degrees seems to be the magic number

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:42.780
according to Apple and Android
phone makers like Samsung.

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:45.960
Apple specifically says here
that exposing your device

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.470
to ambient temperatures higher
than 95 degrees Fahrenheit

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.440
can permanently damage battery capacity.

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:54.750
But that doesn't mean your
smartphone will warn you

00:01:54.750 --> 00:01:57.960
about overheating at 95 degrees.

00:01:57.960 --> 00:02:01.500
I decided to test it myself
by putting this iPhone 13 Pro

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:05.670
and Samsung Galaxy S23
Ultra under a heat lamp.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:06.780
I took the temperature readings

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:08.610
with this FLIR thermal camera

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:11.370
and an external thermometer as a backup.

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:14.610
The first test was just the
phone under the fake sun.

00:02:14.610 --> 00:02:16.320
The alert popped up on the iPhone

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:19.500
after baking for seven
minutes and 20 seconds,

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:21.210
just as the front of
the phone's temperature

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.850
hit 137 degrees.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.010
On the Samsung, an alert
popped up at the phone

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:30.810
was too warm about seven minutes
in when it was 128 degrees.

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:32.220
But unlike Apple's warning,

00:02:32.220 --> 00:02:34.830
Samsung's doesn't limit
you from using the phone.

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.830
It just limits usage of
some parts of the device.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:39.630
The second test, I wanted to know

00:02:39.630 --> 00:02:43.020
if fast charging which
generates heat on its own

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:45.450
would make the temperature rise faster.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:48.300
So I ran the experiment while
the phones were charging

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:51.270
with this special cord that
shows the charging speed.

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:53.250
With both the iPhone and the Samsung,

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:55.440
the phones just slowed the charging speed

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:56.670
as they got warmer.

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:59.790
Oh wait, now it's going
down the charging speed.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:01.410
Both phones had their alerts pop up

00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:06.174
around the same temperature,
between 130 to 135 degrees.

00:03:06.174 --> 00:03:07.590
Ow, very hot.

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:08.760
Very, very hot.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.830
And then there was the final test,

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:13.830
recording some video
while under the hot sun.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:15.510
This really sped up the heat

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:19.710
because recording video is a
very processor intensive task.

00:03:19.710 --> 00:03:23.550
The phone is at about 135 degrees,

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:24.660
and with the video playing,

00:03:24.660 --> 00:03:27.120
it happened in under five minutes.

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.250
So what can you do about all this?

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:31.770
Well, don't put your
phone under a heat lamp,

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:33.900
but sometimes heat happens.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.360
So here are some ideas that I tested.

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:39.030
Number one, buy a bag.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:41.850
I bought this $19 thermal bag on Amazon.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:42.930
I put the phones in it

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:45.390
and then put it under the light and...

00:03:45.390 --> 00:03:46.223
- [Colleague] Oh, no, it's smoking.

00:03:46.223 --> 00:03:47.310
- Okay, yeah, it is, it is.

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:50.160
The thermal bag hit 302 degrees

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:52.110
and we thought, "Let's stop this."

00:03:52.110 --> 00:03:56.700
The phone totally cool, totally
fine inside the thermal bag.

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:59.070
The maker of the bag, a
company called WALLFID

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:01.740
did not respond to my request for comment.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.420
I did try this $30 bag

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:06.090
from a company called
PHOOZY a few days later

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:09.150
and it didn't smoke and
it kept the phone cool.

00:04:09.150 --> 00:04:11.100
So yeah, I'd go with that one.

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:15.120
Number two, limit fast charging and cases.

00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:17.670
Apple and Android phone
makers use software

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:20.850
to balance fast charging
with battery longevity.

00:04:20.850 --> 00:04:23.760
And honestly, I'd say the
convenience of charging fast

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:26.070
is better than babying your battery.

00:04:26.070 --> 00:04:27.810
But if you want to slow down,

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:31.290
go back to this tiny charging
cube if you have an iPhone.

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:35.190
Samsung lets you turn off
fast charging in settings.

00:04:35.190 --> 00:04:37.950
Apple also suggests removing phone cases

00:04:37.950 --> 00:04:40.680
since they can generate
excess heat as well.

00:04:40.680 --> 00:04:42.360
Some Android phones are designed

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:44.400
to better handle fast charging though.

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.820
This OnePlus 10T has
a vapor cooling system

00:04:47.820 --> 00:04:52.230
so it can charge from zero
to 100% in 20 minutes.

00:04:52.230 --> 00:04:54.690
Number three, try a fan.

00:04:54.690 --> 00:04:56.130
Obviously, that bag doesn't work

00:04:56.130 --> 00:04:57.960
if you wanna use your phone.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:01.470
So I tried Razer's $60 phone cooler chroma

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:02.910
which attaches back here.

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:05.250
The only problem with it
is that the fan itself

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:07.080
needs to be plugged in.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:10.620
Still, I was blown away at
how cool it kept the phone.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.450
No temperature warning on this side.

00:05:12.450 --> 00:05:15.450
It has been under the
lamp for about 10 minutes.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:16.830
Look, the good news is

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.390
battery damage isn't gonna just happen

00:05:18.390 --> 00:05:21.300
from that one time you left
the phone on the beach,

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:23.880
but your battery is
gonna degrade over time

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:26.640
and heat will only speed that up.

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:27.473
So stay...

00:05:28.980 --> 00:05:31.680
There's only one place
you're going with this phone.

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:33.783
It's not a rave, it's a land party.

